g that we  just buy more and more as it gets  cheaper more fundamentally the reason  that horse labor disappeared wasn't that  we became satiated with demand for  transportation I'm sure we travel far  more passenger miles now than we did  earlier the real reason was that there  are other technologies that allowed us  to do that and even if we had increased  demand it didn't mean that there'd be  more demand for the horses because other  technologies allowed us to do it which  brings us to another argument which is  this arguments that human needs written  here are two other very famous  economists who make this argument that  sure you satisfy one set of needs but  then there are other needs that become  emerge sources revellings a little play  on words saying that in fact it's not  necessity that they mother of invention  it's an invention that's the mother of  necessity that we will discover things  that we didn't even know we needed until  we start using them after a while  whether it's our iPhone or Facebook or  whatever and this is particularly  powerful and coupled with a fourth  argument that Jim Besson and others have  made again David otter which is that  humans are often indispensable for  certain types of production and in fact  the data shows that although ATMs have  automate a lot of work the number of  bank tellers went up from the 1990s  until 2009 although important footnote  Orca  PostScript to this is since then the  number of bank tellers has started  falling again so so if Jim were to  update his data he would not be able to  use this quote anymore but his  underlying point is an important one  which is that for many tasks if you  automate some of the steps they're still  an indispensable component that requires  human input humans are currently  uniquely good at creativity at  unstructured problem-solving another  category is emotional intelligence and  interpersonal skills many of these jobs  require a human touch a third category  is a lot of 